  "query_preview": "../Sunday through Wednesday/...High pressure over the \nGulf of Alaska will keep the remainder of the weekend dry and \nwarm. Beginning Monday, an impactful Bering Sea low pressure \nsystem tracking east across western and south-central Alaska will \nsend multiple fronts across the region, bringing increasing \nchances of light rain to the northern to north-central panhandle \n(south to approximately Frederick Sound) through Tuesday, with \nprecipitation increasing by late Wednesday, especially for \nYakutat. While 24-hour rainfall totals are not expected to exceed \n1.0\u201d at this time, there is still decent spread in guidance as \nmodels work to resolve this low-high pressure system interaction. \nThe stout Gulf ridge may weaken some as a surface trough \napproaches from the west, allowing for the intrusion of showers. \nNorthwest flow along the ridge axis will transport a moisture \nplume originating from Tropical Storm Lowell into the northern \nregion and could increase wind speeds along exposed prominent \ncapes by Wednesday as the pressure gradient tightens. Increased \ncloud cover in the north will bring slightly cooler afternoon \nhighs alongside mild overnight lows, while the southern panhandle \nstays mostly sunny with highs remaining in the mid to high 60s.",
